  "issue_number": 21,
  "issue_title": "validator hides remote GPU failures by silently falling back to local CPU",
  "issue_body": "## Title\nremote eval silently falls back to local CPU, masking remote GPU execution failures\n\n## Summary\n\n`evaluate_submission()` silently falls back to local CPU when the remote GPU attempt fails.\n\nIn `validator/src/eval_backend/services/eval_runner.py`, any exception in the remote path is captured into `remote_error`, then local evaluation is attempted automatically. If local evaluation succeeds, the run is marked `completed` without clearly surfacing that remote execution failed.\n\n## Impact\n\nThis can hide infrastructure failures and reduce evaluation reproducibility:\n\n- remote GPU outages or config errors can go unnoticed\n- submissions may be evaluated under mixed environments (remote vs local) while appearing equivalent\n- leaderboard/eval trust decreases because execution mode is not strongly signaled\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nMake remote fallback explicit and auditable:\n\n1. Record fallback metadata in `metrics_json` (e.g., `execution_mode: \"local_fallback\"`, `remote_error`).\n2. Include fallback info in `run.message` / `run.phase` so API and PR comments expose degraded mode.\n3. Optionally gate fallback behind an explicit setting (`ALLOW_LOCAL_FALLBACK`) so production can fail fast.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Remote failure + local success is clearly labeled as fallback in persisted run metadata\n- API output and reporting expose that fallback occurred\n- Operators can disable fallback and require strict remote execution\n- Unit tests cover:\n  - remote fail -> local fallback metadata present\n  - remote fail + fallback disabled -> failed run\n  - remote success -> no fallback marker",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-08T21:07:42.000Z",
